From 71c5736b628d1c5f78db10eb2e0c6bc7b6e890e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim 'mithro' Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 22:08:53 +0930
Subject: [PATCH] bcm2835_mphi: deassert the interrupt on reset

mphi_reset() cleared the registers but left the IRQ output as it was.
The interrupt controller keeps the level of its inputs across reset, so
an MPHI interrupt pending when the machine reset stayed asserted into the
next boot.  Raspberry Pi's dwc_otg driver uses MPHI to kick its IRQ from
the FIQ state machine; on raspi0 a reset during USB traffic therefore
left a stale interrupt that fired as soon as the next kernel's dwc_otg
enabled it, before the driver was ready:

- booting stock Raspberry Pi OS (kernel 6.18.50+rpt-rpi-v6) with
  -device usb-net, the third boot (after its second reboot) died with
  "Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address
  000002e8", "PC is at dwc_otg_handle_common_intr+0x574/0x1848",
  "Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception in interrupt";
- reproduced with repeated system_reset during usb-net traffic on the
  firmware repo's 6.18.52+ kernel: the third boot died the same way
  ("PC is at dwc_otg_handle_common_intr+0x37c/0x1818"), in
  dwc_otg_driver_init before its FIQ was set up.

Lower the output in reset.  With this, 13 consecutive resets during
traffic booted cleanly (the next failure was the unrelated system timer
issue fixed separately).
---
 hw/misc/bcm2835_mphi.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/misc/bcm2835_mphi.c b/hw/misc/bcm2835_mphi.c
index 55d79e7..181d2fe 100644
--- a/hw/misc/bcm2835_mphi.c
+++ b/hw/misc/bcm2835_mphi.c
@@ -133,6 +133,12 @@ static void mphi_reset(DeviceState *dev)
     s->ctrl = 0;
     s->intstat = 0;
     s->swirq = 0;
+    /*
+     * Deassert the output too: the interrupt controller keeps the level of
+     * its inputs across reset, so an IRQ pending at reset time would
+     * otherwise stay asserted into the next boot.
+     */
+    mphi_lower_irq(s);
 }
 
 static void mphi_realize(DeviceState *dev, Error **errp)
-- 
